    "totalDuration": "9 yrs 6 mos",
    "employmentType": "Full-time",
    "description": "- Developed a fully custom GPIO Linux kernel driver that dynamically gathers GPIOs from the Device Tree, exposing them to user space via /dev and /sysfs. Enabled scalable board support and streamlined test automation across multiple embedded platforms.\n\n- Created a HWMON driver for the ISL28025 over I2C, exposing current, voltage, power, and temperature metrics to user space via sysfs, which reduced validation effort by 40% and improved system power diagnostics.\n\n- Patched and extended existing Linux kernel drivers to introduce new features and fix critical bugs, improving performance and enabling hardware compatibility for multiple product lines.\n\n- Led board bring-up efforts using U-Boot for custom ARM-based platforms, including DRAM configuration, peripheral initialization, and bootloader optimization for fast boot.\n\n- Designed an internal hardware validation application for factory use, reducing peripheral test time by 30% and streamlining pre-production QA workflows.\n\n- Collaborated closely with hardware and test teams to identify and resolve hardware/software integration issues during bring-up and production phases.\n\n- Debugged kernel and driver-level issues using gdb, oscilloscope traces, and kernel logging techniques to support product reliability and field readiness.\n\n- Followed Linux kernel coding style and upstream submission practices, ensuring maintainability and code quality in all driver work.",
